1 OCCU Överstelöjtnant, Arvlänsherre (=arvsberättigad) till Freienstein, Meyenburg, Neuhausen, Dremnitz och Leddin, Kapten
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Von_Rohr_nr_807
2 CONT
2 CONT Swedish biography: Hans Von Rohr (1484-1569) Arvlänsherre till Freienstein, Meyenburg, Neuhausen, Dremnitz och Leddin. Född 1550-05-08. Inkom 1610 till Livland och blev kapten i svensk tjänst. Sist överstelöjtnant till häst. Död 1
2 CONC 632-11-30 på Svida i Ingermanland. Han förlänades 1619-02-18 av guvernören Carl Carlsson Gyllenhielm med 57 obser ödejord i Ingermanland, vara han jämte Svida pogost år 1628-03-06 erhöll konung Gustaf II Adolfs stadfästelse. 
2 CONT
2 CONT English biography: Hans Von Rohr (1484-1569) was the Lord of Freyenstein Castle, Meyenburg Castle, Neuhausen Castle, Demnitz and Leddin in Brandenburg, Germany. He migrated to Sweden to start the Von Rohrs of Sweden. (b. January 0
2 CONC 1, 1483/1484; Brandenburg, Germany - d. September 07, 1569; Germany or Sweden)
2 CONT
2 CONT Von Rohr: Very old noble family from Mark Brandenburg, where it first shows in the sources with Wolf Orten 1191. From Henning von Rohr, who lived 1287, the family line is known. His grandson Klaus, 1367, registered himself at Meye
2 CONC nburg, and his descendents acquired the estates of Neuhausen, Freienstein, Zernitz, Dremnitz (Tramnitz), Leddin, Trieplatz, and others, all in Brandenburg. The great great grandson of Klaus, Henrik von Rohr who was the lord of Mey
2 CONC enburg in 1448, had the sons Henrik d. 1521 and Alard, who lived 1497, from these the now living German branches stem. Henrik’s decsendents possessed Neuhausen, Freinstein, Meyenburg and others, while the descendents of Alard owne
2 CONC d, among others, Dremnitz, Trieplatz, and Leddin near Prignitz. From Brandenbur the family expanded to Mecklenburg, Pommern, Sachsen, Bohemia, Austria, Sweden and Denmark and other countries. The Swedish branch belongs probably to
2 CONC  the latter line, but no clear evidence. The family was registered at the Finish house of nobility 5 Feb 1818 as no. 63.
