3 TITL Remonstrance to Director Peter Stuyvesant to surrender to British 1664
3 NOTE A.
4 CONT 1656 01 Dec; Abraham Kermer, van Hamburg; Metje Davids, van Aernhem (~•note that Aernhem is in Gelderland ~• Metje Davids is probably the daughter of either Isaac or Tomas Davidz (sic) who we see signing the remonstrance to Pieter
4 CONC  Stuyvesant in September 1655.
4 CONT
4 CONT Keeping in mind that a Thomas Davidszen witnessed the baptism of his fellow country man Denys (or Dionys!) Isaacszen Van Hertevelt's daughter. The connections are between three families : the Davids, the Issacszens, and the van Co
4 CONC rtlands. It is good to look at the document on the Remonstrance of 1665, too, for that is where two Davids, Dionys Issacqs (sic) 
4 CONT
4 CONT 12. (Olaf) Stephanus van Cortlandt (of Wijk bij Duurstede)
4 CONT 21. Tomas Davits (relation to Metje Davids unknown)
4 CONT 47. Dionys Isaacqs (of Wijk bij Durrstede)
4 CONT 49. Arent Isaacqs
4 CONT
4 CONT All these men were from the Gelderland area. Perhaps they were soldiers w/ Van Cortland, this being why they were present at the fort on that fateful day of surrender to England.
3 NOTE Historians have portrayed Stuyvsant as recalcitrant in the surrender of New Amsterdam. It occurs to me that there is a possibility that Petrus wanted to surrender and used this remonstrance to demonstrate to the DWIC that he had c
4 CONC ause to surrender. In other words, he was covering his tracks, so to speak. I say this because so many of his own extended family signed. It does not seem logical that his own family would have "remonstrated" if they knew their pa
4 CONC triarch wanted to fight to the death... as some historians contend.
3 NOTE Nice information for a special new Geni-Project to collect those names together, don't you think @George ?
