2 CONC ://trees.ancestry.com/pt/AMTCitationRedir.aspx?tid=378729&pid=9962
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Roelof was baptized on 1 January 1647 in New Amsterdam, New Netherland.
2 CONT * Roelof's birth, baptism, and death information is available at https://worldconnect.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=4651&id=I27279
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT There was a Roeloff KIERSTEDE (b 01/16/1646/47 d 1713) who married Eyke Aelbert ROOSA (b 04/02/1650 d 1731), who had a descendant by the name of Cornelius KIERSTEDE, who was a gifted silversmith.  I don't know if this was a child 
2 CONC of Roeloff's or perhaps a nephew.  It could be that Cornelius crafted the knives for a ROOSA family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Here is the story on a fabulous bowl crafted by Cornelius KIERSTEDE:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The largest two-handled American Silver Bowl known to date has just been acquired by Robert Ensco, Inc., of New York City. It was made by Cornelius Kierstede, a silversmith of Dutch ancestry, who was baptized in New York in 1675.
2 CONC  He served his apprenticeship as a silversmith and became a Freeman in 1698. He moved to New Haven in 1722.
2 CONT
2 CONT "This Kierstede bowl is unusual because of its size and its beauty. It is ten inches in diameter, four and one-half inches high and weighs a bit over twenty-six ounces. It is more than an inch larger than any other known two-handl
2 CONC ed bowl made by an American silversmith.
2 CONT
2 CONT "There are six panels with embossed floral decoration that suggest the flower motifs used by early Dutch painters. In the bottom of the bowl is a large conventionalized flower of intricate workmanship.
2 CONT
2 CONT "While Cornelius Kierstede worked in New York City his silver was highly individual and showed strong traces of continental influence. Unlike many pieces of fine silver, this two-handled bowl bears the initials of the owners, Q ov
2 CONC er T and V, for Theunis Jacobsen QUICK and his good wife, Vroutje Janse Haring Quick. They were married in the Dutch Reformed Church in New York City, 1st Dec. 1689. Later they moved to Somerset County, N. J., where Theunis was an
2 CONC  honored member of the Dutch Reformed Church in New Brunswick.
2 CONT
2 CONT "There is no question that this bowl was made by Cornelius Kierstede while he still lived in New York City. It shows a welldefined foreign influence with the caryatid handles and the elaborate detail. Stephen G. C. Ensco considers
2 CONC  this piece one of the most important examples of late seventeenth century American silver he has ever seen."
2 CONT
2 CONT This bowl is now in possession of the Metropolitan Museum of Art in New York City.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE:  A Genealogy of the Quick Family in America
2 CONT
2 CONT A picture of this fabulous bowl can be seen at the Metropolitan Museum's website:  http://www.metmuseum.org/collections/view1.asp?dep=1&full=0&item=38%2E63
2 CONT
2 CONT Best regards, Jacquelyn (Erhard) Bean jebean@@ccape.net
