1 NOTE {geni:about_me} notę by Natalia Gurko:
2 CONT
2 CONT = Anna Katarzyna Sanguszko-Radziwiłł (23 September 1676-1746)
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Katarzyna Sanguszko came from the Kowel line of the princely Sanguszko family, one of the old Ruthenian-Lithuanian princely families of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. She was the eldest child of Prince Hieronim Sanguszko
2 CONC  and Konstancja Sapieha.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through her mother, Anna was connected with the powerful Sapieha family. On her father’s side, she was also related to the Radziwiłł family even before her own marriage. Her grandmother, Anna Izabella Radziwiłł, came from the Ołyk
2 CONC a line of the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna was born on 23 September 1676 in Raków, then part of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, now Rakaw in the Minsk Region of Belarus. The estate came into the Sanguszko family through Anna Zawiszanka, who brought Raków into the family
2 CONC  as part of her inheritance.
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known about Anna’s early childhood. Researchers themselves point out that information about this period of her life is scarce. She lost her father at an early age, and after the death of her mother in 1691 she was left a
2 CONC n orphan. Her care was then taken over by Princess Katarzyna Sobieska Radziwiłł, sister of King John III Sobieski and widow of Michał Kazimierz Radziwiłł. She took Anna into her household, and the girl was raised at her court in B
2 CONC iała, today Biała Podlaska. In this way, even before her marriage, Anna was already living within the world of the Radziwiłł family, under the protection of the woman who would soon become her mother-in-law.
2 CONT
2 CONT Katarzyna Sobieska played an important part in arranging Anna’s future. At the age of sixteen, Anna married her guardian’s twenty-three-year-old son, Karol Stanisław Radziwiłł. One biographical publication gives the exact date and
2 CONC  place of the marriage as 6 March 1692 in Vilnius. An earlier source connected with the Sanguszko family and studied by Jakub Rogulski independently confirms the year 1692, although it does not give the exact day or month.
2 CONT
2 CONT An old family genealogy records that Anna, the eldest daughter of Hieronim Sanguszko, married Prince Radziwiłł in 1692. The Polish historian Jakub Rogulski suggests that this information may have been added by Anna herself. She la
2 CONC ter owned an old family prayer book in which the Sanguszko genealogy had been written, and after her marriage she took the book with her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Karol Stanisław secured his young wife’s financial position by granting her a marriage settlement and lifetime rights to the estates of Biała, Sławatycze, Choleszów, Jabłeczna, Czersk and Dołhobrody. The document also stated that 
2 CONC if her husband died, Anna would be free to manage these estates herself. Many years later, this provision became very important and gave her a strong basis for independence as a widow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Katarzyna and Karol Stanisław were married for twenty-seven years. The sources tell us much less about Anna during these years than about the later part of her life. Later authors noted the difference between the characters o
2 CONC f the couple. Karol Stanisław was described as relatively gentle and willing to compromise, while Anna had a strong, determined and commanding personality. It has been suggested that she may already have had considerable influence
2 CONC  over family decisions during her husband’s lifetime, although there is little documentary evidence that would allow us to reconstruct their everyday relationship in detail.
2 CONT
2 CONT The couple had many children. Among them, the most important in later years were Michał Kazimierz Radziwiłł, known by his nickname “Rybeńko”, and Hieronim Florian Radziwiłł. Their daughters included Tekla Róża, Katarzyna Barbara, 
2 CONC Konstancja Franciszka and Karolina Teresa. In later years, the lives of her children, their marriages, offices, property and social position all became part of Anna’s wider family strategy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The turning point in her life came with the death of Karol Stanisław in 1719. He died before he had fully divided his property between his sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Michał Kazimierz was only seventeen years old, while Hieronim Florian was still a child. It was at this point that Anna Katarzyna came to the forefront of family affairs. She effectively took control of the family’s interests and 
2 CONC continued to exercise considerable influence over her sons’ estates even after some of the property had formally passed to them. Michał Kazimierz gave her the necessary authority to manage his affairs in 1721, and Hieronim Florian
2 CONC  did the same in 1733.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was during her years as a widow that Anna’s abilities became most visible. She proved to be an extremely active and demanding estate manager. She supervised local officials, estate administrators and other employees, watched in
2 CONC come and expenditure, checked the condition of the estates and expected her instructions to be followed. Her aim was not simply to preserve the family fortune. She wanted to rebuild and increase the economic strength of the Nesviz
2 CONC h line of the Radziwiłł family.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of her most important concerns was the struggle over the so-called Neuburg estates, a vast inheritance that had become the subject of long disputes over property and dynastic rights. For Anna, this was both a financial and a f
2 CONC amily-political matter. Gaining or securing these estates could directly strengthen the position of the Radziwiłł family among the most powerful magnate houses of the Commonwealth.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband’s death, Anna gradually became the effective leader of the family policy of the Nesvizh line. Modern studies of her correspondence show that even her adult sons did not always act independently of their mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT She made plans, gave instructions, supervised contacts and tried to guide the decisions of her relatives according to the wider interests of the family. For this reason, historians see her not simply as the widow of a great state 
2 CONC official, but as an important figure in her own right, standing behind many of the family’s political, financial and marriage decisions after 1719.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna gave particular attention to the marriages of her children. For her, marriage was a way to increase wealth, build political connections and confirm the high status of the family. She strongly disapproved of marriages that she
2 CONC  considered unsuitable for the position of the Radziwiłłs. She constantly reminded her children that they should form connections with families of equal rank. Family prestige was not an abstract idea for Anna. She saw it as a prac
2 CONC tical political asset.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna had especially ambitious plans for her younger son, Hieronim Florian. She tried to arrange his marriage to Maria Francisca von Sulzbach, granddaughter of the Elector Palatine Charles III Philip of the House of Wittelsbach.
2 CONT
2 CONT Such a marriage would have connected the Radziwiłł family with one of the great European dynasties and might also have helped to settle the question of the Neuburg inheritance. Anna closely followed the negotiations and used her c
2 CONC ontacts at the Mannheim court. When it became clear that the marriage project was failing, she herself ordered her son to leave Mannheim. It is telling that the adult Hieronim Florian obeyed her and wrote that he was ready to wait
2 CONC  for further instructions from his mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna took family ceremonies just as seriously. Weddings, baptisms, name days, birthdays and ceremonial arrivals were, in her eyes, public displays of the family’s position. Modern studies show that the splendour of a ceremony, the
2 CONC  guest list, the presence of important relatives, and even the question of who should or should not attend could have political and reputational importance. The family had not only to possess high status, but also to show that sta
2 CONC tus to others.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna’s involvement in the creation of the Radziwiłł portrait gallery “in a laurel wreath” (w wieńcu laurowym) was closely connected with this idea of family representation. Research directly links the creation of this gallery with
2 CONC  Anna Katarzyna Sanguszko-Radziwiłł. The series of portraits can be seen as another way of creating and preserving the memory of the Radziwiłł family. Portraits of earlier generations created a visible history of the house and emp
2 CONC hasised its ancient roots, continuity and exceptional position. In this sense, the gallery fitted very well with Anna’s wider efforts to shape the public image of the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Modern researchers, however, warn that the gallery should not be treated as a completely reliable source for the appearance or biographies of the people shown in the portraits. The inscriptions under the portraits contain errors a
2 CONC nd inaccuracies whose origins cannot always be identified. Later engravings by Hirsz Leybowicz, based on these portraits, were accompanied by even longer biographical inscriptions. The authorship of these texts has been connected 
2 CONC with Marcin Franciszek Wobe, who supervised the publication of Icones familiae ducalis Radivilianae ex originalibus…. The similarities between the inscriptions under the paintings and those under Leybowicz’s engravings suggest tha
2 CONC t they may have had a common source. These portraits are therefore extremely valuable as evidence of how the Radziwiłł family wished to see and present its own past, but they must be used carefully when establishing specific biogr
2 CONC aphical facts.
2 CONT
2 CONT This aspect of Anna’s activity is particularly interesting when we consider her own family background. She did not forget the memory of the Sanguszko family. One important example is the old prayer book containing a handwritten fa
2 CONC mily genealogy. The book came from the Sanguszko family and was connected with Symeon Samuel Sanguszko. Anna preserved it and took it with her after her marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jakub Rogulski considers it possible that Anna herself added information to the old genealogy about her parents’ marriage, the children of Hieronim Sanguszko and her own marriage to Karol Stanisław. If this attribution is correct,
2 CONC  Anna personally took part in recording the history of her own family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her interest in family memory therefore worked in two directions. She preserved objects connected with her Sanguszko ancestry while at the same time helping to create a visual history of the Radziwiłł family she had entered throug
2 CONC h marriage. This fits well with her understanding of a great family as a dynasty whose position depended not only on wealth and political power, but also on the memory of its origins, ancestors and continuity between generations.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna’s political influence can be seen particularly clearly during the crisis of 1733-1735, which began after the death of Augustus II. Her position did not always agree even with that of her elder son, Michał Kazimierz “Rybeńko”.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the beginning, he supported the Saxon candidate, the future Augustus III, while Anna for some time followed her own political course and maintained contacts with supporters of Stanisław Leszczyński. In the autumn of 1733 she 
2 CONC was among Leszczyński’s circle in Gdańsk, but later changed sides and supported the Saxon party. Her correspondence suggests that her main concerns were the interests of the family, the safety of its estates and the future positio
2 CONC n of the Radziwiłłs under the new political situation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her political style was practical. Even after changing sides, she could maintain useful contacts with people from the opposing camp. During the War of the Polish Succession, Anna closely followed events around Mir, Nesvizh and oth
2 CONC er family estates. She worried about troop movements, taxes and military obligations and tried to use family and client connections to protect the family’s property. She expected her sons to be equally active.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her correspondence also shows how seriously Anna treated political information. She understood the danger of letters falling into the wrong hands and sometimes explicitly ordered correspondence to be destroyed after it had been re
2 CONC ad. For her, family letters were not simply a way for a mother and her children to exchange news. They were also an important tool for managing family politics.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many of Anna’s decisions were based on her understanding of the honour and status of the house. She believed that family members should hold offices appropriate to the rank of the Radziwiłłs, make suitable marriages, maintain the 
2 CONC right family connections and strengthen the reputation of the house through their public behaviour. Property, political influence, marriages, ceremonies and the memory of ancestors were all parts of the same system. Each member of
2 CONC  the family was responsible not only for themselves, but also for the position of the whole house.
2 CONT
2 CONT The religious and cultural world in which Anna grew up also had deep family roots. For several generations, the Kowel line of the Sanguszko family had been connected with church and monastic foundations, charity, books and the pre
2 CONC servation of family heritage. Symeon Samuel Sanguszko was known as a book collector and author. Anna’s interest in old books, genealogy and family memory was therefore not accidental. It was very much part of the traditions of the
2 CONC  family into which she had been born.
2 CONT
2 CONT Taken together, the surviving sources show Anna Katarzyna Sanguszko-Radziwiłł as one of those women of the magnate world whose real influence went far beyond their formal position. During her husband’s lifetime she was less visibl
2 CONC e in the surviving records, but after 1719 she became one of the central figures of the Nesvizh line of the Radziwiłł family. She managed vast estates, supervised their administration, took part in political plans and property dis
2 CONC putes, developed international connections, guided the careers of her sons, became involved in her children’s marriage negotiations and consciously shaped the public image of the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her influence did not come from holding a state office. It was based on her own financial position, her authority as a mother, her birth, her wide network of family connections and her remarkable ability to manage economic, politi
2 CONC cal and family affairs at the same time. Even when Michał Kazimierz and Hieronim Florian had become adult and independent magnates, they remained closely connected with their mother and sometimes acted according to plans she had d
2 CONC eveloped. Studies of her correspondence therefore allow us to see Anna not only as the widow of the Grand Chancellor of Lithuania, but also as a political figure in her own right who could influence the direction taken by the Nesv
2 CONC izh Radziwiłłs.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is perhaps one of the most remarkable aspects of her life. A girl who lost her parents at an early age and was raised in Biała by her future mother-in-law entered one of the most powerful families of the Polish-Lithuanian Com
2 CONC monwealth at the age of sixteen as the wife of Karol Stanisław Radziwiłł. After her husband’s death, she did not withdraw from family affairs or limit herself to the role of mother to adult heirs. Instead, for several decades Anna
2 CONC  remained one of the central figures of the family. She saw the Radziwiłł house almost as a single whole, in which wealth, marriages, political connections, offices, reputation, ceremonies and the memory of ancestors all had to se
2 CONC rve one common purpose: preserving and strengthening the position of the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Based on the following publications:
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Penkała-Jastrzębska, “Publiczne awantury i prywatne interesy. Anna Katarzyna z Sanguszków Radziwiłłowa a sprawy wewnętrzne Rzeczypospolitej w dobie wojny o polską sukcesję (1733-1735)”, Przegląd Historyczny, t. CXIII, 2022, z
2 CONC . 2.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Penkała-Jastrzębska, “W kręgu Anny z Sanguszków Radziwiłłowej. Rola uroczystości prywatnych w polityce kreowania wizerunku rodziny Radziwiłłów”.
2 CONT
